    {
      "id": 230760,
      "name": "der andere Jan",
      "username": "der_andere_Jan",
      "avatar_template": "/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/der_andere_jan/{size}/1881_2.png",
      "created_at": "2020-12-29T23:13:00.000Z",
      "cooked": "<p>After the starter Kit, a lot of minor things were ironed out...</p>\n<p>What we're missing here is: one is a&nbsp;Defend Action (one of the basic action types), and the other is a Reaction&nbsp;that allows you to defend.</p>\n<p>The big&nbsp;distinction to make here is between action (something you do on your turn) and reaction (something you can do anytime, once,&nbsp;between your turns)</p>\n<p>In both cases it means to prep for incoming damage, and mitigate that damage by the result of your roll.</p>\n<p>In&nbsp;a defend *Action*, you collect your pool, roll 3 dice and set up a 'damage sponge' for the next incoming, which is determined by your MID (usually). This is a pre-emptive action, and goes to waste if you are not attacked before your next turn.</p>\n<p>In a defending *REaction*, you do the same, but more hastily, and roll only one die to determine the effectiveness. Thus,&nbsp;results are more random, and it usually appears with a trigger like \"when you would take damage', so the defense&nbsp;will be used up immediately.</p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p>Rule of thumb: On your own turn, (i. e. when you narrate the action), use dice pools. On other's turns, a single roll must suffice, because right now it's not really about you.</p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p>Hope this helps!</p>",
      "post_number": 12,
      "post_type": 1,
      "updated_at": "2021-02-07T06:05:50.873Z",
      "reply_count": 0,
      "reply_to_post_number": null,
      "quote_count": 0,
      "incoming_link_count": 1,
      "reads": 3,
      "readers_count": 2,
      "score": 5.6,
      "yours": false,
      "topic_id": 19921,
      "topic_slug": "what-does-do-you-roll-for-overcome-boost-hinder-and-recover",
      "display_username": "der andere Jan",
      "primary_group_name": null,
      "flair_name": null,
      "flair_url": null,
      "flair_bg_color": null,
